1
00:00:00,470 --> 00:00:07,860
Listen.

2
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:09,040
Practice.

3
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:09,900
Speak.

4
00:00:10,610 --> 00:00:12,230
Practice makes you perfect.

5
00:00:13,930 --> 00:00:18,520
Today, we are really immersing ourselves in the language of business.

6
00:00:18,840 --> 00:00:26,590
Yeah, the shorthand, the sometimes colorful, often confusing phrases that really set the pace in the corporate world.

7
00:00:26,970 --> 00:00:28,650
We're talking about business idioms.

8
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:29,420
Exactly.

9
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:36,830
If you get the strategic point immediately, not just the image of teamwork after dark, you're showing professional maturity.

10
00:00:38,340 --> 00:00:39,500
Okay, so we've seen the value.

11
00:00:39,820 --> 00:00:42,730
Let's jump into the mechanics of work itself, how things get started.

12
00:00:42,750 --> 00:00:48,940
Right, part one, all about motion, starting, keeping going, and knowing when to pause.

13
00:00:49,090 --> 00:00:51,230
Getting things started is obviously critical.

14
00:00:51,610 --> 00:00:56,340
Two big phrases here signal the beginning, though with slightly different feels.

15
00:00:57,060 --> 00:01:00,200
Get the ball rolling and get the show on the road.

16
00:01:00,280 --> 00:01:01,480
Get the ball rolling.

17
00:01:02,010 --> 00:01:05,650
That feels more, I don't know, process-driven, like the source example.

18
00:01:06,050 --> 00:01:08,250
Let's get the ball rolling on the new campaign.

19
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:09,720
It sounds measured.

20
00:01:10,160 --> 00:01:11,360
First step in a sequence.

21
00:01:11,480 --> 00:01:12,960
Yeah, it's like initiating a process.

22
00:01:13,620 --> 00:01:16,870
Whereas get the show on the road, that is a bit more...

23
00:01:16,850 --> 00:01:17,630
Pizazz.

24
00:01:18,250 --> 00:01:18,670
Urgency.

25
00:01:19,050 --> 00:01:21,930
Often for something more physical, like starting a meeting or an event.

26
00:01:21,950 --> 00:01:23,670
Like, okay, planning's done, let's go live.

27
00:01:23,940 --> 00:01:24,380
Exactly.

28
00:01:24,780 --> 00:01:27,480
But just starting isn't the same as starting successfully.

29
00:01:27,780 --> 00:01:29,020
Big difference.

30
00:01:29,040 --> 00:01:30,600
And that's covered by hit the ground running.

31
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:32,850
Ooh, that one comes with baggage.

32
00:01:32,870 --> 00:01:33,870
High expectations.

33
00:01:34,370 --> 00:01:37,330
We expect the new manager to hit the ground running from day one.

34
00:01:37,510 --> 00:01:40,580
It screams immediate competence, no warm-up allowed.

35
00:01:40,680 --> 00:01:42,380
It's the language of peak efficiency, isn't it?

36
00:01:42,820 --> 00:01:43,680
Very aspirational.

37
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:45,280
Demands instant contribution.

38
00:01:45,310 --> 00:01:49,540
So the opposite end of that spectrum might be just trying to get a chance, get a foot in the door.

39
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:53,470
Less about instant success, more about just getting access.

40
00:01:53,870 --> 00:01:54,350
Spot on.

41
00:01:54,570 --> 00:01:56,010
It's about establishing potential.

42
00:01:56,930 --> 00:01:58,630
The internship example nails it.

43
00:01:58,990 --> 00:02:01,800
This internship will help you get a foot in the door.

44
00:02:02,180 --> 00:02:02,660
Right.

45
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:07,610
The internship itself might be small, but strategically, it's huge.

46
00:02:08,070 --> 00:02:10,090
It's that first point of contact, the way in.

47
00:02:10,190 --> 00:02:11,250
OK, so we've started.

48
00:02:11,390 --> 00:02:12,560
Now keep things moving.

49
00:02:12,700 --> 00:02:14,220
That's keep the ball rolling.

50
00:02:14,240 --> 00:02:14,640
Exactly.

51
00:02:14,660 --> 00:02:15,750
It's the continuous version.

52
00:02:15,970 --> 00:02:20,060
If you've launched the campaign, now you need everyone's effort to keep the ball rolling.

53
00:02:20,660 --> 00:02:22,670
It highlights that momentum isn't automatic.

54
00:02:22,770 --> 00:02:23,590
It needs fuel.

55
00:02:23,910 --> 00:02:25,950
And sometimes that fuel means working late.

56
00:02:26,470 --> 00:02:29,770
The classic burning the midnight oil or burn the midnight oil.

57
00:02:29,810 --> 00:02:31,360
Such a great image, isn't it?

58
00:02:31,380 --> 00:02:32,700
Pre-electricity, hard work.

59
00:02:33,160 --> 00:02:36,060
We had to burn the midnight oil to meet the project deadline.

60
00:02:36,340 --> 00:02:36,860
Classic.

61
00:02:37,060 --> 00:02:38,620
But there's a nuance there, isn't there?

62
00:02:38,970 --> 00:02:43,930
Is it celebrating hard work or is it maybe a subtle complaint about poor planning?

63
00:02:44,090 --> 00:02:45,730
That's the strategic read you have to make.

64
00:02:45,990 --> 00:02:47,290
Context is everything.

65
00:02:47,940 --> 00:02:53,540
Is it a one-off heroic effort or is this just how things always are, which points to a bigger problem?

66
00:02:53,810 --> 00:02:54,130
Right.

67
00:02:54,210 --> 00:03:04,360
And it connects to keep your nose to the grindstone, which feels more like sustained, disciplined effort over time, less frantic, more grinding.

68
00:03:04,440 --> 00:03:04,780
True.

69
00:03:05,220 --> 00:03:07,650
And often that effort is driven by deadlines.

70
00:03:07,830 --> 00:03:10,430
We talk about working against the clock or being down to the wire.

71
00:03:10,670 --> 00:03:11,610
Time becomes the enemy.

72
00:03:11,650 --> 00:03:12,360
Completely.

73
00:03:12,380 --> 00:03:13,560
You're under serious pressure.

74
00:03:13,940 --> 00:03:15,500
Every second feels critical.

75
00:03:15,980 --> 00:03:19,510
It signals a high stakes, maybe risky situation.

76
00:03:19,570 --> 00:03:20,550
Thankfully, we can stop.

77
00:03:20,590 --> 00:03:22,350
The simple end of day is call it a day.

78
00:03:22,370 --> 00:03:23,740
Like finish the presentation.

79
00:03:23,860 --> 00:03:24,580
Let's call it a day.

80
00:03:25,180 --> 00:03:25,740
Nice and easy.

81
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:28,710
But stopping a project needs different language.

82
00:03:28,730 --> 00:03:33,250
If it's delayed, maybe indefinitely, but not canceled, you put it on the back burner.

83
00:03:33,270 --> 00:03:34,190
Like simmering soup.

84
00:03:34,650 --> 00:03:36,180
Still there, but not the focus.

85
00:03:36,380 --> 00:03:36,960
Exactly.

86
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:43,500
The redesign project example, putting it on the back burner for now means it's paused strategically, not dead.

87
00:03:43,720 --> 00:03:47,550
But if it is dead or failing badly, the language gets tougher.

88
00:03:47,850 --> 00:03:48,590
Pull the plug.

89
00:03:48,570 --> 00:03:49,570
Yeah, that sounds final.

90
00:03:49,710 --> 00:03:51,070
Like stopping life support.

91
00:03:51,290 --> 00:03:53,810
Management decided to pull the plug on the underperforming project.

92
00:03:54,190 --> 00:03:56,880
It's about cutting losses, stopping the resource drain.

93
00:03:57,240 --> 00:03:58,160
Quick, decisive.

94
00:03:58,560 --> 00:04:01,180
And then there's the social stop, the polite postponement.

95
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:02,160
Take a rain check.

96
00:04:02,620 --> 00:04:03,990
Essential for relationships.

97
00:04:04,010 --> 00:04:04,370
Totally.

98
00:04:04,690 --> 00:04:06,050
Canceling lunch because of a meeting.

99
00:04:06,270 --> 00:04:07,310
I'll have to take a rain check.

100
00:04:07,810 --> 00:04:08,610
Softens the blow.

101
00:04:08,950 --> 00:04:10,670
Implies you do want to reschedule.

102
00:04:10,690 --> 00:04:12,000
It's politeness wrapped up.

103
00:04:12,210 --> 00:04:14,030
OK, so part one was about action.

104
00:04:14,220 --> 00:04:16,820
Part two is making sure everyone's moving together.

105
00:04:17,120 --> 00:04:20,790
Communication, clarity, misalignment kills projects.

106
00:04:20,990 --> 00:04:21,690
Right.

107
00:04:21,710 --> 00:04:23,400
So these phrases are crucial.

108
00:04:23,640 --> 00:04:24,480
Absolutely foundational.

109
00:04:24,940 --> 00:04:26,760
We start with getting everyone thinking alike.

110
00:04:26,980 --> 00:04:27,930
Two close cousins here.

111
00:04:27,990 --> 00:04:30,790
Be on the same wavelength and on the same page.

112
00:04:30,770 --> 00:04:36,760
Wave 1 feels more conceptual, intuitive, ensure we're on the same wavelength before moving forward.

113
00:04:36,780 --> 00:04:38,920
Like, do we share the basic vision?

114
00:04:39,040 --> 00:04:50,130
Yeah, it's often about the underlying understanding, whereas on the same page usually relates to something more concrete, specific facts, a plan, maybe even looking at the same document.

115
00:04:50,540 --> 00:04:52,480
Are we all seeing this strategy the same way?

116
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:53,540
It's a tactical check.

117
00:04:53,560 --> 00:04:57,740
And if there's definite disagreement, that's see eye to eye or usually not seeing eye to eye.

118
00:04:57,820 --> 00:04:58,080
Right.

119
00:04:58,550 --> 00:05:04,730
The source points out when teams don't see eye to eye on campaign strategies, it means there's friction.

120
00:05:05,710 --> 00:05:08,460
A clear disagreement that probably needs sorting out.

121
00:05:08,560 --> 00:05:10,020
Okay, agreement established.

122
00:05:10,040 --> 00:05:16,990
Now, keeping everyone informed, up to speed, and in the loop, I feel like one's a one-off, the other's continuous.

123
00:05:17,210 --> 00:05:18,110
That's a great way to put it.

124
00:05:18,370 --> 00:05:20,730
Bringing someone up to speed is a specific action.

125
00:05:21,110 --> 00:05:23,830
Like, let's get everyone up to speed on the new marketing strategy.

126
00:05:23,930 --> 00:05:24,950
Boom, you're informed.

127
00:05:25,210 --> 00:05:25,540
Got it.

128
00:05:25,960 --> 00:05:28,600
But being in the loop means you're constantly kept informed.

129
00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:30,460
Keep me in the loop about any timeline changes.

130
00:05:30,580 --> 00:05:30,920
Yeah.

131
00:05:30,940 --> 00:05:31,880
It's an ongoing status.

132
00:05:32,240 --> 00:05:34,170
The VP needs bringing up to speed.

133
00:05:34,190 --> 00:05:35,530
The legal team stays in the loop.

134
00:05:35,660 --> 00:05:36,080
Makes sense.

135
00:05:36,240 --> 00:05:39,700
And for newcomers, they need to know the ropes or learn the ropes.

136
00:05:40,220 --> 00:05:42,550
That sounds like needing deep practical knowledge.

137
00:05:42,790 --> 00:05:44,510
It's that institutional wisdom, isn't it?

138
00:05:44,550 --> 00:05:46,510
Not just the manual, how things really work.

139
00:05:46,910 --> 00:05:48,980
Our source links it to experience.

140
00:05:49,060 --> 00:05:52,240
Someone with five years under his belt knows the rope.

141
00:05:52,640 --> 00:05:53,560
Ah, under his belt.

142
00:05:54,200 --> 00:05:57,450
Another body metaphor meaning accumulated proven experience.

143
00:05:58,030 --> 00:05:58,510
Nice link.

144
00:05:58,530 --> 00:05:58,710
Right.

145
00:05:59,070 --> 00:06:01,110
So shifting gears, brevity.

146
00:06:01,630 --> 00:06:02,390
Business runs on it.

147
00:06:02,570 --> 00:06:03,560
No time for rambling.

148
00:06:03,690 --> 00:06:06,470
If time's money, these phrases are like quick transactions.

149
00:06:06,730 --> 00:06:07,170
Exactly.

150
00:06:07,310 --> 00:06:10,290
If someone says, cut to the chase, they're taking control.

151
00:06:10,670 --> 00:06:12,320
Get to the point now.

152
00:06:12,960 --> 00:06:14,800
It's a direct command for efficiency.

153
00:06:15,260 --> 00:06:16,100
Then the summarizers.

154
00:06:16,480 --> 00:06:17,260
Long story.

155
00:06:17,540 --> 00:06:18,720
Make a long story short.

156
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:19,840
Like, hmm?

157
00:06:19,860 --> 00:06:20,730
Make a long story short.

158
00:06:20,750 --> 00:06:21,850
We need more resources.

159
00:06:22,210 --> 00:06:23,270
Gets you the headline fast.

160
00:06:23,510 --> 00:06:24,430
And the ultimate summary.

161
00:06:24,970 --> 00:06:26,670
In a nutshell, the executive brief.

162
00:06:27,290 --> 00:06:30,380
In the nutshell, we need better cross-department communication.

163
00:06:30,640 --> 00:06:32,060
Quick, clear, actionable.

164
00:06:32,140 --> 00:06:33,720
OK, now controlling information.

165
00:06:34,140 --> 00:06:35,520
Secrecy, sources.

166
00:06:35,940 --> 00:06:38,290
Sometimes info is shared, but privately.

167
00:06:38,310 --> 00:06:39,710
Off the record.

168
00:06:39,910 --> 00:06:40,630
Critical phrase.

169
00:06:41,050 --> 00:06:43,350
Media, politics, internal chats.

170
00:06:43,370 --> 00:06:45,190
It means this is not for public consumption.

171
00:06:45,270 --> 00:06:45,810
Don't quote me.

172
00:06:46,200 --> 00:06:49,560
Like the example he told me off the record about possible company changes.

173
00:06:49,960 --> 00:06:52,080
Allows sensitive discussion without causing panic.

174
00:06:52,200 --> 00:06:55,710
And if the whole project is being kept quiet, that's under the radar.

175
00:06:55,790 --> 00:06:55,970
Yep.

176
00:06:56,230 --> 00:06:58,930
Keep this initiative under the radar until we finalize details.

177
00:06:59,510 --> 00:07:01,330
Strategic silence.

178
00:07:01,310 --> 00:07:05,740
avoids competitor attention, prevents internal jitters before things are said.

179
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:07,990
On the flip side, proving truthfulness.

180
00:07:08,470 --> 00:07:10,510
Claiming it's straight from the horse's mouth.

181
00:07:10,610 --> 00:07:11,910
The ultimate source credential.

182
00:07:12,490 --> 00:07:14,620
Means you heard it directly from the person in charge.

183
00:07:15,100 --> 00:07:17,560
Heard straight from the horse's mouth, the project's extended.

184
00:07:17,920 --> 00:07:18,660
Cuts through rumor.

185
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:19,870
And the danger zone.

186
00:07:20,190 --> 00:07:20,510
Yeah.

187
00:07:20,530 --> 00:07:21,610
Secrets getting out early.

188
00:07:21,870 --> 00:07:22,470
The fun ones.

189
00:07:22,790 --> 00:07:24,890
Spill the beans and let the cat out of the bag.

190
00:07:25,010 --> 00:07:28,340
Both mean revealing something, usually by accident or too soon.

191
00:07:28,840 --> 00:07:33,190
Someone spilled beans about the new product or let the cat out of the bag about the merger.

192
00:07:33,450 --> 00:07:33,970
Whoops.

193
00:07:33,990 --> 00:07:34,470
Big whoops.

194
00:07:35,330 --> 00:07:36,190
The damage is done.

195
00:07:36,250 --> 00:07:43,050
The sheer physicality of those images, beans spilling, a cat escaping, really highlights how irreversible it is.

196
00:07:43,300 --> 00:07:45,940
All right, part three, facing the tough stuff.

197
00:07:46,400 --> 00:07:48,830
Problems, conflicts, staying strong.

198
00:07:49,470 --> 00:07:52,170
These idioms get pretty expressive here dealing with stress.

199
00:07:52,350 --> 00:07:52,810
Yeah, they do.

200
00:07:53,050 --> 00:07:54,650
First step, spotting the problem.

201
00:07:55,190 --> 00:07:59,060
If you hit a snag, it's usually a setback, an obstacle, often later in a process.

202
00:07:59,640 --> 00:08:02,600
Like hitting a snag during the final stages of a product launch.

203
00:08:02,620 --> 00:08:04,460
Annoying, but probably fixable.

204
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:05,800
But sometimes it's not a snag.

205
00:08:05,820 --> 00:08:07,520
It's bigger, much bigger.

206
00:08:07,900 --> 00:08:10,290
The thing everyone knows is wrong but avoids talking about?

207
00:08:10,750 --> 00:08:11,750
The elephant in the room.

208
00:08:11,770 --> 00:08:14,690
That one's huge, literally and figuratively.

209
00:08:15,560 --> 00:08:18,320
Our sources mention it twice, which tells you how common it is.

210
00:08:19,100 --> 00:08:21,110
The budget crisis, the looming layoffs.

211
00:08:21,150 --> 00:08:22,670
The awkward silence topic.

212
00:08:22,790 --> 00:08:23,270
Exactly.

213
00:08:23,710 --> 00:08:26,420
Saying, let's address the elephant in the room.

214
00:08:26,890 --> 00:08:28,610
The buddy it cuts takes guts.

215
00:08:29,150 --> 00:08:30,980
It forces the difficult conversation.

216
00:08:31,040 --> 00:08:33,040
And sometimes the problem you see is just the surface.

217
00:08:33,100 --> 00:08:34,460
That's the tip of the iceberg.

218
00:08:34,500 --> 00:08:34,620
Right.

219
00:08:34,640 --> 00:08:36,110
Those initial customer complaints.

220
00:08:36,730 --> 00:08:39,380
Probably just the tip of the iceberg of a deeper issue.

221
00:08:39,940 --> 00:08:40,560
It's a warning.

222
00:08:41,480 --> 00:08:42,320
Don't be complacent.

223
00:08:42,340 --> 00:08:44,530
The real problem is much larger underneath.

224
00:08:44,670 --> 00:08:51,260
And when problems corner you personally, you're in a tight spot or between a rock and a hard place.

225
00:08:51,580 --> 00:08:58,670
Both mean you're stuck, facing a difficult choice between two bad options, often driven by deadlines or budget constraints.

226
00:08:59,250 --> 00:09:00,040
No easy way out.

227
00:09:00,580 --> 00:09:05,680
And if the problem itself is just really hard to figure out or solve, it's a tough nut to crack.

228
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:06,180
Yeah.

229
00:09:06,470 --> 00:09:10,350
Describes the nature of the challenge itself, like complex client needs that keep changing.

230
00:09:10,450 --> 00:09:13,860
It's about the inherent difficulty, not just the situation you're in.

231
00:09:13,870 --> 00:09:17,250
Okay, so when things go wrong, someone has to take responsibility.

232
00:09:17,480 --> 00:09:19,060
Leaders might need to take the heat.

233
00:09:19,340 --> 00:09:23,130
Means accepting the blame, the criticism, often for the team.

234
00:09:23,950 --> 00:09:25,430
The source example is perfect.

235
00:09:25,990 --> 00:09:27,480
I'll take the heat for the project delays.

236
00:09:27,500 --> 00:09:28,540
It was my responsibility.

237
00:09:29,140 --> 00:09:29,960
That's accountability.

238
00:09:30,680 --> 00:09:31,830
Can shield others too.

239
00:09:32,160 --> 00:09:33,680
But sometimes it's not about blame.

240
00:09:33,710 --> 00:09:36,090
It's about doing something unpleasant but necessary.

241
00:09:36,110 --> 00:09:38,160
That's bite the bullet.

242
00:09:38,400 --> 00:09:38,620
Right.

243
00:09:38,980 --> 00:09:44,230
Doing the hard thing you have to do, even if you hate it, like addressing budget cuts or making difficult staff changes.

244
00:09:44,820 --> 00:09:47,400
It's about necessary, painful action.

245
00:09:47,470 --> 00:09:52,490
And if you don't do it or things just fail anyway, then you have to face the music.

246
00:09:52,690 --> 00:09:53,940
Yeah, that's consequence time.

247
00:09:54,300 --> 00:09:59,760
Dealing with the fallout, the judgment, especially if the project isn't delivered on time, sounds like a formal reckoning.

248
00:09:59,940 --> 00:10:02,610
And after the music, no point dwelling on it.

249
00:10:02,710 --> 00:10:04,150
That's cry over spilled milk.

250
00:10:04,310 --> 00:10:04,790
Classic.

251
00:10:04,890 --> 00:10:06,590
Being upset about something you can't change.

252
00:10:06,930 --> 00:10:07,850
Used to force a shift.

253
00:10:08,410 --> 00:10:09,760
No use crying over spilled milk.

254
00:10:09,780 --> 00:10:10,260
Let's fix it.

255
00:10:10,920 --> 00:10:11,720
Pushes you forward.

256
00:10:11,700 --> 00:10:14,220
Which leads us nicely into resilience.

257
00:10:14,240 --> 00:10:15,640
Business loves resilience.

258
00:10:15,680 --> 00:10:17,430
If things are tough, keep your chin up.

259
00:10:17,870 --> 00:10:19,330
Basic psychological boost, right?

260
00:10:19,930 --> 00:10:21,610
Stay positive despite the setbacks.

261
00:10:22,120 --> 00:10:23,420
Manage your mindset.

262
00:10:23,440 --> 00:10:26,340
And sometimes what looks like a failure turns out good.

263
00:10:27,300 --> 00:10:28,920
Every cloud has a silver lining.

