00:20:56,160 --> 00:20:56,590
Makes sense.

528
00:20:57,090 --> 00:21:00,850
And the Q&A management, always a tricky one in business presentations.

529
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:01,580
Crucial.

530
00:21:02,020 --> 00:21:03,620
You need to manage it up front.

531
00:21:04,160 --> 00:21:09,830
When you say something clear like, please feel free to ask questions at the end of the presentation, you're setting boundaries.

532
00:21:10,490 --> 00:21:11,690
Firm, but polite.

533
00:21:11,910 --> 00:21:12,840
So you control the flow.

534
00:21:13,160 --> 00:21:13,760
Exactly.

535
00:21:13,920 --> 00:21:15,460
You're signaling professionalism.

536
00:21:15,520 --> 00:21:22,430
You're making sure your strategic narrative doesn't get derailed by a minor question about, I don't know, a footnote on slide three.

537
00:21:22,570 --> 00:21:22,790
Right.

538
00:21:23,170 --> 00:21:26,140
But you're also assuring them that there will be time for their questions.

539
00:21:26,440 --> 00:21:28,820
It's the diplomatic way to say, hold that thought.

540
00:21:28,920 --> 00:21:29,620
We'll get there.

541
00:21:30,120 --> 00:21:31,110
maintains control.

542
00:21:32,670 --> 00:21:32,730
OK.

543
00:21:32,750 --> 00:21:33,650
So the intro is done.

544
00:21:33,690 --> 00:21:35,030
The audience knows the plan.

545
00:21:35,830 --> 00:21:37,940
Now you need these general purpose statements.

546
00:21:38,040 --> 00:21:44,270
Think of them as verbal signposts throughout the main body of the presentation little flags along the way.

547
00:21:44,390 --> 00:21:45,310
Yeah exactly.

548
00:21:45,330 --> 00:21:51,460
They're the tools you use to refocus attention especially when you're shifting between maybe quite different topics or points.

549
00:21:51,440 --> 00:21:52,900
So these are like your verbal highlighters.

550
00:21:52,940 --> 00:21:58,030
They pull the audience's eye or ear rather to the really critical stuff.

551
00:21:58,370 --> 00:22:05,120
We saw some strong examples like let's focus on the key takeaways from this presentation or even more direct.

552
00:22:05,160 --> 00:22:08,910
Maybe I'll highlight the most critical points for your reference.

553
00:22:09,010 --> 00:22:12,950
And what those sentences do is they implicitly promise efficiency.

554
00:22:13,620 --> 00:22:19,910
You're basically telling the audience, pay attention now, this next bit is distilled, high priority information.

555
00:22:19,950 --> 00:22:21,270
Right, cuts through the noise.

556
00:22:21,410 --> 00:22:25,680
And that connects directly to summarizing and contextualizing.

557
00:22:25,660 --> 00:22:52,070
let's say you've just shown a really dense chart lots of market data you can't just click to the next slide no you need to bridge it you need to ground that data put it in context of the bigger strategy something like this slide summarizes the main findings of our analysis that's synthesis it shows you've done the thinking process the raw data and here's the conclusion and building credibility here is key you have to link your conclusions or recommendations back to the evidence

558
00:22:52,100 --> 00:22:52,830
Absolutely.

559
00:22:53,210 --> 00:22:58,260
Using phrases like these recommendations are based on detailed market research that shows rigor.

560
00:22:58,400 --> 00:23:02,490
It tells them your proposals aren't just, you know, gut feelings or opinions.

561
00:23:02,600 --> 00:23:03,720
They're fact based.

562
00:23:03,940 --> 00:23:05,400
Demonstrates diligence.

563
00:23:05,520 --> 00:23:05,840
Yes.

564
00:23:06,380 --> 00:23:09,770
And this is also where you start to introduce the language of future action.

565
00:23:10,530 --> 00:23:14,360
Sometimes you do this even before you get to the specific recommendation slides.

566
00:23:14,540 --> 00:23:15,300
Interesting.

567
00:23:15,320 --> 00:23:15,560
Why?

568
00:23:15,580 --> 00:23:17,780
It keeps the momentum going forward.

569
00:23:18,140 --> 00:23:24,820
It keeps the focus shifting towards solutions rather than dwelling too long on the problems or the analysis itself.

570
00:23:24,940 --> 00:23:25,180
Okay.

571
00:23:25,200 --> 00:23:25,960
Give me an example.

572
00:23:25,940 --> 00:23:31,510
A perfect one from the sources was, moving forward, our priority will be streamlining processes.

573
00:23:32,150 --> 00:23:32,330
See?

574
00:23:32,370 --> 00:23:39,350
You're signaling the direction, the strategic why, before you even reveal the specific how.

575
00:23:39,510 --> 00:23:39,730
Right.

576
00:23:39,750 --> 00:23:41,740
It sets the stage for the solutions to come.

577
00:23:41,720 --> 00:23:42,360
Exactly.

578
00:23:42,640 --> 00:23:46,370
And what's interesting there, too, is the expectation it sets for internal alignment.

579
00:23:46,910 --> 00:23:52,220
You might follow that sentence up with something like this initiative will require cross department collaboration.

580
00:23:52,760 --> 00:23:53,200
Ah, OK.

581
00:23:53,340 --> 00:23:56,330
So you're preempting the not my problem reaction.

582
00:23:56,430 --> 00:23:57,030
Precisely.

583
00:23:57,350 --> 00:24:00,720
It stops people thinking, oh, that's an ops issue or that's for sales.

584
00:24:00,990 --> 00:24:05,830
It subtly forces everyone in the room to start internalizing that this is a collective responsibility.

585
00:24:06,490 --> 00:24:07,370
Shared ownership.

586
00:24:07,590 --> 00:24:08,990
Okay, let's shift gears.

587
00:24:09,100 --> 00:24:11,640
We've set the stage, outlined the journey.

588
00:24:12,280 --> 00:24:14,160
Now we get to the core content journey.

589
00:24:14,900 --> 00:24:18,160
The numbers, the data, spreadsheets made visible.

590
00:24:18,260 --> 00:24:19,930
The heart of the QPR, usually.

591
00:24:20,090 --> 00:24:26,890
And the big challenge here is how do you take data, which can be inherently, let's face it, dry,

592
00:24:26,870 --> 00:24:34,100
and make it engaging, make it understandable for an audience that's smart and informed, but also really pressed for time.

593
00:24:34,280 --> 00:24:36,550
Yeah, this transition is absolutely key.

594
00:24:36,590 --> 00:24:38,530
You have to move beyond just showing the data.

595
00:24:38,630 --> 00:24:41,660
You need to be actively narrating the story behind the numbers.

596
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:43,320
Storytelling with data.

597
00:24:43,480 --> 00:24:44,220
In essence, yes.

598
00:24:44,540 --> 00:24:48,370
The audience needs to grasp not just what the number is, but what it actually means.

599
00:24:48,810 --> 00:24:53,600
What does it signify for strategic success or, you know, where course correction is needed?

600
00:24:53,680 --> 00:24:53,880
Right.

601
00:24:54,060 --> 00:24:54,840
So how do we start?

602
00:24:55,180 --> 00:24:56,960
Language for just displaying the data.

603
00:24:56,940 --> 00:24:58,390
You want to invite the audience in.

604
00:24:58,450 --> 00:25:01,250
Use accessible language when you introduce charts or graphs.

605
00:25:01,290 --> 00:25:03,620
Make them feel like active evidence supporting your points.

606
00:25:03,800 --> 00:25:05,540
So avoid just saying, here's a graph.

607
00:25:05,800 --> 00:25:06,640
Please avoid that.

608
00:25:06,660 --> 00:25:10,590
Instead, use more professional, inviting phrases.

609
00:25:11,070 --> 00:25:14,300
Things like, let's take a closer look at the data from this quarter.

610
00:25:14,340 --> 00:25:15,580
Or...

611
00:25:15,560 --> 00:25:18,800
This graph clearly illustrates our customer attention rates.

612
00:25:19,400 --> 00:25:22,190
That framing suggests you're investigating it together.

613
00:25:22,210 --> 00:25:23,930
Creates a sense of shared exploration.

614
00:25:23,950 --> 00:25:24,510
Exactly.

615
00:25:24,570 --> 00:25:26,840
That shared investigation idea is powerful.

616
00:25:27,480 --> 00:25:31,080
Then you move into quantifying the achievements, the components.

617
00:25:31,500 --> 00:25:34,490
You combine that visual language with very specific figures.

618
00:25:35,150 --> 00:25:37,090
Focus on the precise wins.

619
00:25:37,460 --> 00:25:38,120
For example.

620
00:25:38,140 --> 00:25:41,490
Something like, as you can see, we achieved 90% of our sales target.

621
00:25:41,930 --> 00:25:45,230
You're using the visual, as you can see, as immediate proof.

622
00:25:45,250 --> 00:25:48,180
It directly links the team's effort to a measurable result.

623
00:25:48,320 --> 00:25:50,420
And the details really matter in a QPR, don't they?

624
00:25:50,700 --> 00:25:51,320
Specificity.

625
00:25:51,600 --> 00:25:52,060
Absolutely.

626
00:25:52,100 --> 00:25:55,390
The source material really stressed highlighting specific wins clearly.

627
00:25:55,690 --> 00:26:00,620
For instance, our top performing region was North America with a 12% increase in sales.

628
00:26:01,380 --> 00:26:05,660
Or perhaps revenue from our premium products increased significantly this quarter.

629
00:26:05,800 --> 00:26:06,980
That's precise language.

630
00:26:07,040 --> 00:26:09,900
It avoids vague stuff like, sales looked pretty good.

631
00:26:09,960 --> 00:26:10,430
Right.

632
00:26:10,790 --> 00:26:17,060
It focuses attention on what specifically drove success, which gives a model maybe for replication elsewhere.

633
00:26:17,260 --> 00:26:17,980
Provides learnings.

634
00:26:18,200 --> 00:26:18,420
Yes.

635
00:26:19,070 --> 00:26:24,060
And for the bigger picture, you need phrases that break down the total contribution accurately.

636
00:26:24,560 --> 00:26:35,350
A sentence like the pie chart shows the contribution of each region to total revenue ensures everyone understands the relative weight, the importance of different business units or areas.

637
00:26:35,470 --> 00:26:37,010
So you're displaying the components clearly.

638
00:26:37,150 --> 00:26:40,860
Which sets the stage for the more detailed financial analysis that might follow.

639
00:26:41,400 --> 00:26:48,290
It prevents people from, say, overestimating the impact of a smaller region just because it had high percentage growth.

640
00:26:48,990 --> 00:26:50,030
Context is key.

641
00:26:50,010 --> 00:26:56,570
OK, displaying is one thing, but the analysis, the interpretation, that's where the real value comes in, right?

642
00:26:56,650 --> 00:26:58,640
Moving from what happened to why it happened.

643
00:26:58,660 --> 00:27:00,260
Which is where it gets really sophisticated, yes.

644
00:27:00,540 --> 00:27:02,960
And you need the right language to navigate this smoothly.

645
00:27:03,000 --> 00:27:10,690
You need to show progress, but also identify gaps, often in the same presentation without it descending into a blame game.

646
00:27:10,930 --> 00:27:11,690
Tricky balance.

647
00:27:11,710 --> 00:27:12,050
Very.

648
00:27:12,570 --> 00:27:14,490
Let's look at positive attribution first.

649
00:27:14,780 --> 00:27:16,120
How do you frame success?

650
00:27:16,920 --> 00:27:22,730
Sentences like, these results demonstrate strong progress in our key markets, work well.

651
00:27:23,010 --> 00:27:25,130
They affirm the strategy and the execution.

652
00:27:25,830 --> 00:27:26,770
Okay, more specific.

653
00:27:26,790 --> 00:27:29,040
Even better is linking effort to outcome.

654
00:27:30,200 --> 00:27:33,520
Our marketing efforts contributed significantly to the growth in North America.

655
00:27:33,720 --> 00:27:39,140
See, that connects a specific controllable action the marketing spend directly to the success.

656
00:27:39,750 --> 00:27:43,150
It implicitly justifies that budget allocation for the future.

657
00:27:43,290 --> 00:27:44,870
Makes the case for more investment there.

658
00:27:45,070 --> 00:27:45,970
Potentially, yes.

659
00:27:46,230 --> 00:27:51,080
But dealing with the setbacks, the misses, that's where the language skills are truly tested.

660
00:27:51,220 --> 00:27:51,420
Yeah.

661
00:27:51,440 --> 00:27:53,140
How do you talk about failure professionally?

662
00:27:53,520 --> 00:27:57,530
When you identify gaps, the language needs to be precise, but also neutral.

663
00:27:57,670 --> 00:28:00,590
You want to avoid sounding defensive or like you're just passing the buck.

664
00:28:01,390 --> 00:28:02,970
Consider the structure of the sentence.

665
00:28:03,210 --> 00:28:06,660
The decline in Europe can be attributed to increased competition.

666
00:28:06,800 --> 00:28:07,720
OK, break that down.

667
00:28:07,760 --> 00:28:09,320
What's powerful about that phrasing?

668
00:28:09,490 --> 00:28:10,870
It's got two key elements.

669
00:28:11,550 --> 00:28:17,160
First, it often uses a passive construction can be attributed, which subtly depersonalizes the issue.

670
00:28:17,270 --> 00:28:19,830
It's not we failed, it's this decline happened.

671
00:28:20,190 --> 00:28:25,300
Second, in this example, it points to an external factor, increased competition.

672
00:28:25,280 --> 00:28:36,920
Now, that's quite masterful communication wise, because it redirects the focus away from internal finger pointing, whose fault was it, towards external factors and how to mitigate them.

673
00:28:37,440 --> 00:28:42,430
It frames it as the market environment changed rather than our team messed up.

674
00:28:42,510 --> 00:28:45,560
That structural choice is subtle, but really impactful.

675
00:28:45,780 --> 00:28:48,450
It lets critical thinking about solutions begin immediately.

676
00:28:48,650 --> 00:28:49,150
Exactly.

677
00:28:49,170 --> 00:28:51,370
You also mentioned nuanced interpretation.

678
00:28:51,430 --> 00:28:58,070
That's vital, especially when maybe the top line numbers look OK, but the underlying efficiency or profitability isn't quite there.

679
00:28:58,150 --> 00:28:58,290
Right.

680
00:28:58,310 --> 00:29:00,980
The headline looks good, but the details are worrying.

681
00:29:00,960 --> 00:29:01,500
Precisely.

682
00:29:01,680 --> 00:29:06,570
Look at a phrase like, while sales increased, our profit margins were slightly lower than expected.

683
00:29:07,150 --> 00:29:09,510
That is the language of executive level rigor.

684
00:29:09,630 --> 00:29:10,230
Also.

685
00:29:10,250 --> 00:29:13,360
It signals immediately that you're not just celebrating the revenue growth.

686
00:29:13,690 --> 00:29:17,600
You're digging deeper, analyzing the efficiency, the overall financial health.

687
00:29:18,140 --> 00:29:23,970
That single sentence flags a need for caution for more investigation, even during what looks like a growth phase.

688
00:29:24,090 --> 00:29:25,630
It shows you're on top of the details.

689
00:29:25,610 --> 00:29:28,770
And the interpretation, it always has to point towards action, right?

690
00:29:28,790 --> 00:29:30,250
The necessary next step.

691
00:29:30,530 --> 00:29:32,280
The data can't just hang there.

692
00:29:32,360 --> 00:29:34,070
It absolutely dictates the agenda.

693
00:29:34,140 --> 00:29:35,690
The source gave a very clear example.

694
00:29:36,230 --> 00:29:39,490
The data suggests that we need to focus on improving delivery times in Asia.

695
00:29:40,030 --> 00:29:42,680
The analysis flows directly into a proposed action.

696
00:29:43,320 --> 00:29:46,120
Problem identified, potential solution flagged.

697
00:29:46,220 --> 00:29:46,640
Makes sense.

698
00:29:47,040 --> 00:29:51,890
And finally, distinguishing between internal and external factors is crucial for context.

699
00:29:52,410 --> 00:29:55,400
It completely shapes the kind of strategy you'll propose later.

700
00:29:55,980 --> 00:29:57,020
How do you mean?

701
00:29:57,290 --> 00:30:03,600
If the analysis points internally, like, the numbers indicate a need for better coordination between teams.

702
00:30:04,100 --> 00:30:05,280
That's a controllable issue.

703
00:30:05,360 --> 00:30:06,780
It's an optimization problem.

704
00:30:07,020 --> 00:30:11,090
You fix it with process changes, better communication protocols, that sort of thing.

705
00:30:11,450 --> 00:30:18,240
But if the analysis points externally, like, the challenges in Europe are primarily due to economic factors.

706
00:30:18,860 --> 00:30:20,610
That's much less controllable directly.

707
00:30:20,970 --> 00:30:22,230
It's a mitigation issue.

708
00:30:22,820 --> 00:30:27,420
You address it through maybe risk avoidance, hedging strategies, market diversification.

709
00:30:27,590 --> 00:30:27,910
Got it.

710
00:30:28,150 --> 00:30:33,360
So the language used in the analysis directly dictates the type of strategic response needed later.

711
00:30:33,600 --> 00:30:34,040
Exactly.

712
00:30:34,440 --> 00:30:34,540
Yeah.

713
00:30:34,560 --> 00:30:35,760
That distinction is fundamental.

714
00:30:36,000 --> 00:30:36,240
All right.

715
00:30:36,500 --> 00:30:37,860
Now we need to elevate the discussion.

716
00:30:38,500 --> 00:30:49,500
We're moving past the general performance metrics and into the really critical specific language you need when you're talking about money, about the future strategic direction, and importantly, about potential risks.

717
00:30:49,550 --> 00:30:52,030
This feels like the language of executive accountability.

718
00:30:52,150 --> 00:30:53,030
It absolutely is.

719
00:30:53,050 --> 00:30:54,080
It demands precision.

720
00:30:54,100 --> 00:30:56,380
You have to include specific financial metrics.

721
00:30:56,820 --> 00:30:58,140
No room for vagueness here.

722
00:30:58,160 --> 00:30:58,480
Okay.

723
00:30:58,500 --> 00:31:02,510
So for financial performance statements, anchoring in concrete numbers is key.

724
00:31:02,530 --> 00:31:05,410
You have to highlight figures that really demonstrate financial health.

725
00:31:05,630 --> 00:31:05,890
Yes.

726
00:31:06,350 --> 00:31:07,530
Focus on growth, for example.

727
00:31:07,890 --> 00:31:11,100
Our total revenue increased by 8% compared to the previous quarter.

728
00:31:11,640 --> 00:31:13,700
Quantifiable success, specific timeframe.

729
00:31:14,020 --> 00:31:14,240
Clear.

730
00:31:14,680 --> 00:31:18,390
And sometimes efficiency is even more important than just raw growth, right?

731
00:31:18,490 --> 00:31:19,810
Often, yes.

732
00:31:19,830 --> 00:31:21,330
Especially regarding profitability.

733
00:31:21,770 --> 00:31:24,280
So you need phrases detailing resource management.

734
00:31:24,780 --> 00:31:29,080
Things like, operating costs have decreased by 5%, improving our profit margins.

735
00:31:29,300 --> 00:31:30,000
That's huge.

736
00:31:30,120 --> 00:31:33,770
Or another critical one, our customer acquisition cost has dropped by 10%.

737
00:31:33,890 --> 00:31:34,230
Yes.

738
00:31:34,990 --> 00:31:38,110
If you're in marketing or operations, those are your power statements.

739
00:31:38,590 --> 00:31:43,460
They directly link the efficiency of your spending, your operations, to the company's bottom line.

740
00:31:44,020 --> 00:31:46,300
That's music to a CFO's ears.

741
00:31:46,700 --> 00:31:51,700
We also need language to break down where the money's coming from, revenue streams, market penetration.

742
00:31:52,070 --> 00:31:54,930
Crucial for understanding dependencies and opportunities.

743
00:31:55,570 --> 00:32:02,380
For example, to highlight reliance on a key product, sales of our flagship product accounted for 60% of total revenue.

744
00:32:02,440 --> 00:32:04,780
Okay, that immediately flags the risk, doesn't it?

745
00:32:05,120 --> 00:32:06,350
If that one product falters.

746
00:32:06,510 --> 00:32:07,050
Exactly.

747
00:32:07,230 --> 00:32:08,810
It highlights concentration risk.

748
00:32:08,830 --> 00:32:12,470
And when discussing expansion, market share in Asia increased by 3%.

749
00:32:13,330 --> 00:32:16,800
Again, specific, measurable progress in a target area.

750
00:32:16,920 --> 00:32:18,260
What about recurring revenue?

751
00:32:18,320 --> 00:32:19,680
That's a big one these days.

752
00:32:19,830 --> 00:32:23,670
Huge, especially for subscription businesses or sauce companies.

753
00:32:24,260 --> 00:32:28,700
We should clarify for the listener, we're talking monthly recurring revenue, MRR.

754
00:32:29,640 --> 00:32:34,210
It's that predictable income stream that often heavily influences a company's valuation.

755
00:32:34,890 --> 00:32:35,010
Right.

756
00:32:35,030 --> 00:32:41,440
So a phrase like monthly recurring revenue is now at an all-time high is a powerful statement.

757
00:32:41,460 --> 00:32:45,140
It speaks to stability, predictability, sustainable growth.

758
00:32:45,430 --> 00:32:49,540
And when you talk about the bottom line, the net profit, specificity is king.

759
00:32:49,940 --> 00:32:52,580
Vague language just raises doubts.

760
00:32:52,600 --> 00:32:52,960
Totally.

761
00:32:53,060 --> 00:32:54,830
The source material was emphatic about this.

762
00:32:54,950 --> 00:32:55,690
Be specific.

763
00:32:56,130 --> 00:32:58,750
Net profit for the quarter reached $2.3 million.

764
00:32:59,210 --> 00:33:00,020
Use the actual number.

765
00:33:00,300 --> 00:33:01,820
It's not just about sounding professional.

766
00:33:02,060 --> 00:33:04,310
That's the language of verifiable accountability.

767
00:33:04,770 --> 00:33:06,930
Anything less sounds like you're hiding something.

768
00:33:07,030 --> 00:33:08,310
Okay, financials covered.

769
00:33:08,550 --> 00:33:11,600
But a balanced presentation needs the flip side, risk.

770
00:33:12,060 --> 00:33:13,500
You can't just celebrate success.

771
00:33:13,780 --> 00:33:15,940
You need to show foresight about potential problems.

772
00:33:15,960 --> 00:33:18,790
Risk analysis is the necessary counterbalance, absolutely.

773
00:33:19,030 --> 00:33:23,080
A truly strategic presentation must demonstrate awareness of potential hazards.

774
00:33:23,100 --> 00:33:26,140
And the language here needs to be just as precise as the financial language.

775
00:33:26,480 --> 00:33:29,830
So how do you articulate these potential challenges clearly?

776
00:33:29,860 --> 00:33:33,410
Well, you start by identifying the internal challenges.

777
00:33:34,090 --> 00:33:37,800
These are often structural issues, things management can actually take steps to fix.

778
00:33:37,960 --> 00:33:42,090
For example, delays in production have been a recurring issue this quarter.

779
00:33:42,350 --> 00:33:45,170
OK, that acknowledges an ongoing internal problem.

780
00:33:45,190 --> 00:33:46,420
It demands attention.

781
00:33:46,450 --> 00:33:46,810
Yes.

782
00:33:46,910 --> 00:33:49,430
It signals we know this is happening and it needs fixing.

783
00:33:49,890 --> 00:33:55,080
Then you have the external factors, the ones you adapt to or mitigate rather than fix directly.

784
00:33:55,260 --> 00:33:56,200
Like market conditions.

785
00:33:56,340 --> 00:33:56,830
Exactly.

786
00:33:56,880 --> 00:34:01,510
Phrases like rising raw material costs could impact our profit margins.

787
00:34:01,630 --> 00:34:02,770
That's a clear warning sign.

788
00:34:03,470 --> 00:34:05,620
Or the broader, less controllable stuff.

789
00:34:06,460 --> 00:34:10,030
Economic instability in certain regions remains a concern.

790
00:34:10,600 --> 00:34:14,780
These require hedging or maybe strategic shifts in sourcing or market focus.

791
00:34:14,860 --> 00:34:16,320
But you can't just list threats.

792
00:34:16,420 --> 00:34:18,170
You need to frame them constructively.

793
00:34:18,290 --> 00:34:18,490
Right.

794
00:34:18,630 --> 00:34:22,010
You need to frame these vulnerabilities as areas for proactive planning.

795
00:34:22,430 --> 00:34:24,670
Turn the threat into a trigger for strategic action.

796
00:34:25,050 --> 00:34:28,000
The source had some great phrases around market adaptation.

797
00:34:28,220 --> 00:34:32,400
Market trends suggest a possible decline in demand for traditional products.

798
00:34:32,380 --> 00:34:33,700
See how that's phrased.

799
00:34:34,320 --> 00:34:35,730
It's a gentle but firm warning.

800
00:34:36,350 --> 00:34:44,400
It implies that preemptive investment in new areas or pivoting the existing product line is needed before the market forces your hand.

801
00:34:44,540 --> 00:34:46,250
Proactive, not reactive.

802
00:34:46,270 --> 00:34:46,810
Exactly.

803
00:34:47,030 --> 00:34:48,470
And what about safeguarding internally?

804
00:34:48,930 --> 00:34:50,770
Things like cybersecurity.

805
00:34:50,850 --> 00:34:51,760
Critical these days.

806
00:34:51,780 --> 00:34:52,240
Absolutely.

807
00:34:52,280 --> 00:34:53,480
So a sentence like...

808
00:34:53,760 --> 00:35:03,320
Data security must be a top priority to avoid breaches, takes what might seem like a technical IT issue and elevates it to a strategic imperative for the whole business.

809
00:35:03,700 --> 00:35:07,010
Everyone needs to understand how vital protecting company assets is.

810
00:35:07,330 --> 00:35:11,290
So the overall tone for risk needs to convey readiness, agility.

811
00:35:11,530 --> 00:35:12,820
Agility is the perfect word.

812
00:35:13,000 --> 00:35:17,640
The phrase, we need to stay agile in an evolving market, sums it up nicely.

813
00:35:17,620 --> 00:35:20,550
It's a call to action that covers all sorts of unforeseen changes.

814
00:35:20,610 --> 00:35:22,580
But the sources also mentioned the opposite, right?

815
00:35:22,740 --> 00:35:24,200
The cost of not being agile.

816
00:35:24,580 --> 00:35:24,820
Yes.

817
00:35:25,240 --> 00:35:27,310
And that's powerful negative reinforcement.

818
00:35:27,890 --> 00:35:33,520
Understanding that failure to adapt quickly to market changes could result in lost opportunities.

819
00:35:33,940 --> 00:35:39,130
Sometimes highlighting the potential loss, the cost of inaction, is the strongest motivator for change.

820
00:35:39,670 --> 00:35:42,820
You need language that underscores the risk of standing still.

821
00:35:42,990 --> 00:35:44,060
OK, let's talk innovation.

822
00:35:44,740 --> 00:35:51,350
Investing in tech, R&D, that often means big budgets, significant capital expenditure.

823
00:35:51,430 --> 00:35:52,930
Huge investments, often.

824
00:35:52,980 --> 00:35:56,700
So the language you use here has to justify that spend, right?

825
00:35:56,720 --> 00:36:00,350
It needs to directly link the innovation effort to measurable business results.

826
00:36:00,370 --> 00:36:00,870
Absolutely.

827
00:36:00,930 --> 00:36:04,130
It has to be clear ROI language, return on investment.

828
00:36:04,490 --> 00:36:11,620
For instance, instead of just saying we're implementing AI, which is vague, the source suggests connecting it directly to an operational improvement.

829
00:36:12,260 --> 00:36:16,010
Implementing AI tools has streamlined our customer service operations.

830
00:36:16,330 --> 00:36:20,670
The focus is squarely on the result, the benefit, not just the technology itself.

831
00:36:20,650 --> 00:36:23,140
And quantifying that benefit makes it even stronger.

832
00:36:23,480 --> 00:36:24,100
Much stronger.

833
00:36:24,300 --> 00:36:27,770
Like, automation has reduced production costs by 10%.

834
00:36:28,150 --> 00:36:32,880
That's a direct line from the tech investment to the bottom line to profitability.

835
00:36:33,540 --> 00:36:34,320
Hard to argue with.

836
00:36:34,960 --> 00:36:39,330
You can also highlight impact on the customer side, maybe user behavior.

837
00:36:39,410 --> 00:36:42,080
Yes, validating the product team's work, perhaps.

838
00:36:42,320 --> 00:36:45,580
Our new app feature has significantly boosted user engagement.

839
00:36:46,180 --> 00:36:50,790
That shows the investment is resonating with the market, improving the customer experience.

840
00:36:50,860 --> 00:36:57,390
So ultimately, these kinds of statements frame technology not just as a cost center, but as a strategic necessity.

841
00:36:57,410 --> 00:36:57,850
Exactly.

842
00:36:57,950 --> 00:37:00,850
It's about protecting the company's future competitive position.

843
00:37:01,490 --> 00:37:11,080
A sentence like investing in R&D is critical for maintaining a competitive edge ties the company's long term survival and success directly to those current investment decisions.

844
00:37:11,340 --> 00:37:12,990
Positions the company is forward looking.

845
00:37:13,120 --> 00:37:15,580
And defensively postured against market disruption.

846
00:37:15,780 --> 00:37:16,860
It's essential framing.

847
00:37:17,020 --> 00:37:17,240
All right.

848
00:37:17,260 --> 00:37:18,070
We've done the analysis.

849
00:37:18,110 --> 00:37:21,490
We've looked at the financials, identified risks, talked tech investment.

850
00:37:21,890 --> 00:37:23,330
Now we hit the crucial pivot point.

851
00:37:23,970 --> 00:37:24,660
Recommendations.

852
00:37:25,480 --> 00:37:25,740
Action.

853
00:37:25,900 --> 00:37:27,660
Moving from diagnosis to prescription.

854
00:37:28,100 --> 00:37:28,560
Exactly.

855
00:37:28,900 --> 00:37:32,730
The focus shifts entirely from the language of problems to the language of solutions.

856
00:37:33,110 --> 00:37:37,010
And this language needs to be direct, decisive, really actionable.

857
00:37:37,100 --> 00:37:41,300
Yes, the language for recommendations needs strong, action-oriented verbs.

858
00:37:41,940 --> 00:37:43,700
These aren't just passive suggestions anymore.

859
00:37:43,740 --> 00:37:44,780
They are proposals.

860
00:37:45,460 --> 00:37:48,370
Management is looking for leadership here, for clear direction.

861
00:37:48,690 --> 00:37:49,370
Okay.

862
00:37:49,390 --> 00:37:52,210
So for market-based solutions, how should that sound?

863
00:37:52,230 --> 00:37:52,750
Decisive.

864
00:37:53,050 --> 00:37:56,780
For example, to address these challenges, we propose a new pricing strategy.

865
00:37:57,420 --> 00:37:58,700
Clear action proposed.

866
00:37:59,380 --> 00:38:00,520
Or maybe more tactical.

867
00:38:00,960 --> 00:38:03,660
Offering discounts in Europe could attract more customers.

868
00:38:04,020 --> 00:38:06,990
It clearly states the action and the intended outcome.

869
00:38:07,000 --> 00:38:09,460
And operationally, focus on efficiency.

870
00:38:09,720 --> 00:38:10,300
Often, yes.

871
00:38:10,690 --> 00:38:14,390
Things like partnering with local suppliers will improve our supply chain efficiency.

872
00:38:15,160 --> 00:38:16,820
Or linking back to tech investment.

873
00:38:17,300 --> 00:38:20,370
Investing in automation will reduce operational costs.

874
00:38:20,480 --> 00:38:22,650
Notice the verbs will improve, will reduce.

875
00:38:23,290 --> 00:38:25,630
It expresses confidence in the proposed outcome.

876
00:38:25,850 --> 00:38:25,990
Right.

877
00:38:26,010 --> 00:38:26,740
It sounds definitive.

878
00:38:27,160 --> 00:38:27,680
It needs to.

879
00:38:27,720 --> 00:38:30,380
And defining priorities is essential here, too.

880
00:38:30,960 --> 00:38:33,110
With limited resources, where should the focus be?

881
00:38:33,450 --> 00:38:34,170
You need to guide that.

882
00:38:34,550 --> 00:38:35,090
Absolutely.

883
00:38:35,130 --> 00:38:36,470
Internally, it might be.

884
00:38:36,510 --> 00:38:41,320
Introducing new training programs for the sales team is a top priority.

885
00:38:41,300 --> 00:38:44,750
That acknowledges investing in people is key for success.

886
00:38:45,380 --> 00:38:46,360
Externally, perhaps.

887
00:38:46,880 --> 00:38:49,850
A focus on customer feedback will guide our next steps.

888
00:38:50,370 --> 00:38:55,480
That anchors the future strategy in what the market actually wants, ensuring relevance.

889
00:38:55,990 --> 00:38:59,310
Now, a QPR isn't just about spreadsheets and strategies.

890
00:38:59,750 --> 00:39:06,010
It's fundamentally about the people, the teams who delivered those results or who need to execute the new plan.

891
00:39:06,090 --> 00:39:06,310
Right.

892
00:39:06,390 --> 00:39:07,190
The human element.

893
00:39:07,230 --> 00:39:08,050
Hugely important.

894
00:39:08,070 --> 00:39:15,200
So you need to use language that recognizes effort, motivates people, and ultimately secures the buy-in from those who have to make it happen next quarter.

895
00:39:15,760 --> 00:39:19,850
So celebrating successes, even small ones, is important for morale.

896
00:39:20,080 --> 00:39:21,810
Vital for morale and retention.

897
00:39:22,030 --> 00:39:23,690
The source highlighted specific examples.

898
00:39:23,830 --> 00:39:25,770
The sales team exceeded their target by 10%.

899
00:39:25,950 --> 00:39:28,440
Clear, quantifiable team success.

900
00:39:28,960 --> 00:39:31,300
And it's just as important to recognize the support functions.

901
00:39:31,760 --> 00:39:35,050
The IT team resolved technical issues, quickly minimizing downtime.

902
00:39:35,290 --> 00:39:38,940
Make sure everyone feels seen, especially the teams working behind the scenes.

903
00:39:38,970 --> 00:39:39,770
Exactly.

904
00:39:39,810 --> 00:39:43,190
Ensures no department feels overlooked or undervalued.

905
00:39:43,750 --> 00:39:48,620
And the language should also actively foster a positive high performance culture.

906
00:39:48,640 --> 00:39:51,460
Well, using motivational phrases strategically.

907
00:39:52,220 --> 00:39:55,910
Celebrating small wins keeps the team motivated and engaged.

908
00:39:55,990 --> 00:39:56,930
That's not just fluff.

909
00:39:56,950 --> 00:39:57,930
It's a management tool.

910
00:39:58,570 --> 00:40:03,250
And foundational statements like open communication is vital for maintaining team morale.

911
00:40:03,230 --> 00:40:09,470
reinforce a culture where people feel safe to speak up, which is critical when you're discussing failures or need to pivot quickly.

912
00:40:09,570 --> 00:40:10,570
Psychological safety.

913
00:40:10,730 --> 00:40:11,230
Precisely.

914
00:40:11,710 --> 00:40:16,600
And I think we should also emphasize the language around shared success, particularly collaboration.

915
00:40:16,720 --> 00:40:17,700
Breaking down silos.

916
00:40:18,090 --> 00:40:22,030
Yes, highlighting process improvements achieved through collaboration.

917
00:40:22,660 --> 00:40:26,220
Collaboration between teams has significantly improved our workflows.

918
00:40:26,840 --> 00:40:34,640
That specifically points out the value of working together, or emphasizing that cross-functional meetings have enhanced project coordination.

919
00:40:35,300 --> 00:40:42,710
This shows management is actively working to improve how work gets done, not just demanding more output from the existing structure.

920
00:40:43,360 --> 00:40:48,970
Okay, so every recommendation, every discussion about teams, it all has to lead somewhere concrete, future goals.

921
00:40:48,990 --> 00:40:50,030
Right, the what's next.

922
00:40:50,050 --> 00:40:53,820
And these goals must be measurable and ideally time bound.

923
00:40:53,980 --> 00:40:55,280
We need to quantify success.

924
00:40:55,440 --> 00:40:57,740
Everyone needs to know exactly what hitting the target looks like.

925
00:40:57,860 --> 00:40:58,360
Absolutely.

926
00:40:58,440 --> 00:41:00,350
Vague goals lead to vague results.

927
00:41:00,590 --> 00:41:02,690
The source had good examples here too, right?

928
00:41:02,710 --> 00:41:03,850
Quantified objectives.

929
00:41:03,900 --> 00:41:04,860
Excellent examples.

930
00:41:04,960 --> 00:41:08,300
Instead of just saying we need to grow in Asia, which is too fuzzy.

931
00:41:08,430 --> 00:41:08,910
Make it smart.

932
00:41:09,390 --> 00:41:10,150
Exactly.

933
00:41:10,190 --> 00:41:15,110
Make it specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, time bound.

934
00:41:15,550 --> 00:41:20,520
Like our primary goal for next quarter is to increase market share in Asia.

935
00:41:21,100 --> 00:41:24,730
Specific marmot, specific metric or for customer focus.

936
00:41:25,300 --> 00:41:28,640
We aim to improve customer retention by 10% over the next year.

937
00:41:29,200 --> 00:41:31,610
Quantified target, clear timeframe.

938
00:41:31,720 --> 00:41:34,830
And these short-term goals, they need to connect to the bigger picture.

939
00:41:35,410 --> 00:41:37,170
Crucial for alignment and motivation.

940
00:41:37,750 --> 00:41:41,400
You need to explicitly link these quarterly pushes to the long-term vision.

941
00:41:42,100 --> 00:41:48,050
Using phrases like achieving cost efficiency remains a top priority reinforces a consistent strategic theme.

942
00:41:48,650 --> 00:41:52,130
Or concluding with these results align with our long-term goals.

943
00:41:52,110 --> 00:41:54,160
That ensures the team sees the relevance, right?

944
00:41:54,460 --> 00:41:58,860
How their intense effort this quarter actually contributes to the company's multi-year strategy.

945
00:41:58,940 --> 00:41:59,390
Precisely.

946
00:41:59,630 --> 00:42:05,320
It connects the day-to-day grind to the overarching vision, hugely important for keeping people engaged and focused on the right things.

947
00:42:05,680 --> 00:42:08,740
All right, we're nearing the end of the presentation itself, the final few minutes.

948
00:42:08,800 --> 00:42:11,450
And honestly, this wrap-up is almost as important as the opening.

949
00:42:11,470 --> 00:42:12,330
How you land the plane.

950
00:42:12,310 --> 00:42:13,190
Exactly.

951
00:42:13,370 --> 00:42:23,310
This is where you exit smoothly, you reinforce the key messages, solidify confidence, and then crucially, you transition from presenting mode into a productive discussion mode.

952
00:42:23,530 --> 00:42:24,590
Collaboration time.

953
00:42:24,650 --> 00:42:25,250
Yes.

954
00:42:25,270 --> 00:42:27,770
So first, the closing statements themselves.

955
00:42:28,390 --> 00:42:31,200
They need to synthesize things and project confidence.

956
00:42:31,180 --> 00:42:33,660
Leave them with a positive, actionable feeling.

957
00:42:33,740 --> 00:42:33,970
Right.

958
00:42:34,410 --> 00:42:37,410
The summer should be concise, maybe focusing on the potential.

959
00:42:37,780 --> 00:42:43,130
To summarize, our performance this quarter shows great potential, and that forward look needs conviction.

960
00:42:43,850 --> 00:42:46,940
We're confident that these steps will lead to continued success.

961
00:42:47,310 --> 00:42:48,630
You need to sound like you believe it.

962
00:42:48,870 --> 00:42:52,200
You absolutely do, and it's critical to define the next steps clearly.

963
00:42:52,780 --> 00:42:55,140
Don't let the momentum fizzle out when the slides go dark.

964
00:42:55,160 --> 00:42:56,040
Keep the ball rolling.

965
00:42:56,220 --> 00:42:56,720
Exactly.

966
00:42:57,140 --> 00:42:59,080
Commitment and follow-up language is vital.

967
00:42:59,260 --> 00:43:02,670
Something like, following through on these recommendations is crucial.

968
00:43:02,870 --> 00:43:06,750
And the practical part, let's schedule a follow-up meeting to track progress.

969
00:43:07,410 --> 00:43:11,400
That creates accountability for both the presenter and the management team listening.

970
00:43:11,720 --> 00:43:13,760
And maybe a final motivational push.

971
00:43:13,780 --> 00:43:16,200
We saw some language aimed at collective ownership.

972
00:43:16,350 --> 00:43:24,600
Yeah, closing that loop on the QPR being a shared effort is powerful, ending with something like, together we can achieve even better results next quarter.

973
00:43:25,460 --> 00:43:30,230
It rallies the team, uses unifying language, sets a positive tone for the period ahead.

974
00:43:31,210 --> 00:43:32,510
Okay, presentation finished.

975
00:43:33,270 --> 00:43:33,910
Now the Q&A.

976
00:43:34,950 --> 00:43:37,080
The standard handover is necessary, of course.

977
00:43:37,940 --> 00:43:39,700
I'd like to open the floor for questions now.

978
00:43:40,220 --> 00:43:41,360
Simple, standard.

979
00:43:41,680 --> 00:43:44,270
But the source suggested something more proactive, right?

980
00:43:44,630 --> 00:43:46,610
Not just passively waiting for questions.

981
00:43:47,050 --> 00:43:49,500
Yes, and I think this is a fantastic insight.

982
00:43:50,020 --> 00:43:52,240
The most effective presenters don't just wait.

983
00:43:52,570 --> 00:43:54,750
They actively drive critical dialogue.

984
00:43:54,930 --> 00:44:00,360
They use specific strategic questions themselves rather than just hoping someone asks something insightful.

985
00:44:00,450 --> 00:44:00,930
Interesting.

986
00:44:00,950 --> 00:44:04,330
So instead of just any questions, you prompt deeper thinking.

987
00:44:04,470 --> 00:44:04,990
Exactly.

988
00:44:05,030 --> 00:44:07,670
Use the kinds of questions suggested in our source material.

989
00:44:07,870 --> 00:44:09,530
Focusing on feasibility, for example.

990
00:44:09,680 --> 00:44:12,760
Asking directly, do you think these recommendations are feasible?

991
00:44:13,100 --> 00:44:16,160
Ah, that immediately pulls in feedback from the people who actually have to do it.

992
00:44:16,400 --> 00:44:16,920
Precisely.

993
00:44:17,260 --> 00:44:24,330
It solicits constructive input right away, potentially flagging bottlenecks or resource constraints before they become major problems.

994
00:44:24,630 --> 00:44:24,950
Smart.

995
00:44:25,630 --> 00:44:27,200
What about focusing on obstacles?

996
00:44:27,460 --> 00:44:34,850
Equally essential, asking something like, what challenges do you foresee with implementing this plan or maybe framing it collaboratively?

997
00:44:35,570 --> 00:44:38,500
Let's discuss any potential obstacles you anticipate.

998
00:44:38,860 --> 00:44:39,580
What does that signal?

999
00:44:39,920 --> 00:44:47,070
It shows the management team is grounded in reality, prepared to deal with difficulties, not just operating on optimistic assumptions.

1000
00:44:47,190 --> 00:44:51,330
It actually builds trust because you're acknowledging things might be tough.

1001
00:44:51,350 --> 00:44:54,780
And connecting the strategy back to individuals, to departments.

1002
00:44:54,800 --> 00:44:55,020
Right.

1003
00:44:55,040 --> 00:44:56,880
You mentioned that for securing buy-in.

1004
00:44:56,960 --> 00:45:01,990
Key for getting real commitment, asking directly, how do you see this impacting your department?

1005
00:45:02,530 --> 00:45:07,480
Or maybe more personally, how do you see these plans impacting your day-to-day operations?

1006
00:45:07,540 --> 00:45:09,060
Makes it relevant, makes it personal.

1007
00:45:09,180 --> 00:45:15,550
Yes, and it forces functional leaders to actually commit resources mentally, if not verbally, right then and there.

1008
00:45:15,970 --> 00:45:17,910
It secures that deeper level of buy-in.

1009
00:45:18,110 --> 00:45:20,640
And finally, showing openness to alternatives.

1010
00:45:20,840 --> 00:45:26,500
Important for fostering innovation and signaling that the strategy isn't set in stone, that it's a collaborative process.

1011
00:45:27,100 --> 00:45:31,290
Asking, could we explore alternative solutions to this particular issue?

1012
00:45:31,330 --> 00:45:35,110
Invites contribution without undermining the work already done.

1013
00:45:35,510 --> 00:45:38,530
So the ultimate goal of the Q&A isn't just answering questions.

1014
00:45:38,920 --> 00:45:40,780
No, it's achieving final alignment.

1015
00:45:41,160 --> 00:45:44,740
It's perfectly summed up by that closing participation phrase we saw.

1016
00:45:45,160 --> 00:45:47,580
Let's make sure everyone is aligned before we move forward.

1017
00:45:47,660 --> 00:45:48,680
That's the objective.

1018
00:45:48,750 --> 00:45:49,210
Wow.

1019
00:45:49,590 --> 00:45:49,870
Okay.

1020
00:45:50,470 --> 00:46:11,060
We covered a ton of ground today, ran through those essential sentence structures for really impactful QPRs, breaking it down by function, that crucial introduction, the rigorous data and analysis piece, the specialized language for financial strategy and risk, and then bringing it home with actionable language for goals, recommendations, and the conclusion.

1021
00:46:11,040 --> 00:46:12,440
It's a comprehensive toolkit.

1022
00:46:12,520 --> 00:46:13,080
It really is.

1023
00:46:13,560 --> 00:46:23,980
And mastering these structures, understanding the why behind them, that feels like the fastest way to genuinely elevate your business English, to go from just being informative to being truly influential.

1024
00:46:24,360 --> 00:46:24,760
Agreed.

1025
00:46:25,520 --> 00:46:30,990
And perhaps we can leave our listeners with a final thought, connecting a couple of key points from our dive today.

1026
00:46:31,170 --> 00:46:31,690
Let's do it.

1027
00:46:31,870 --> 00:46:32,950
A provocative takeaway.

1028
00:46:33,060 --> 00:46:33,320
Okay.

1029
00:46:34,010 --> 00:46:40,460
So we talked about the financial success that comes from, say, focusing on high margin products has improved profitability.

1030
00:46:40,880 --> 00:46:41,380
That's a win.

1031
00:46:41,840 --> 00:46:44,650
It's based on current market realities demonstrated success.

1032
00:46:44,810 --> 00:46:45,130
Right.

1033
00:46:45,150 --> 00:46:45,970
Celebrate that.

1034
00:46:46,360 --> 00:46:56,560
But we also spent time on that vital risk assessment language, particularly the warning that failure to adapt quickly to market changes could result in lost opportunities.

1035
00:46:57,200 --> 00:46:58,180
The agility piece.

1036
00:46:58,420 --> 00:47:03,010
OK, so success based on today's reality versus the need to adapt for tomorrow's.

1037
00:47:03,690 --> 00:47:04,290
See where you're going.

1038
00:47:04,430 --> 00:47:04,890
Exactly.

1039
00:47:04,970 --> 00:47:06,720
So imagine this scenario for yourself.

1040
00:47:06,910 --> 00:47:12,980
You've just presented a killer QPR celebrating huge success driven by that high margin product strategy.

1041
00:47:13,440 --> 00:47:14,420
Everyone's feeling good.

1042
00:47:14,460 --> 00:47:15,950
Champagne corks are ready to pop.

